UNIT oracle_diff
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@4c134cacd842 cli/lsp/explain.mjs oracleDiff and shown, with the canon.mjs display and canonicalString they call — the code is the truth, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG oracle_diff(before: text, after: text) -> record[before: text, after: text, added: list[record[name: text, hidden: bool, input: text, to: text]], removed: list[record[name: text, hidden: bool, input: text, to: text]], rewritten: list[record[name: text, hidden: bool, input: text, from: text, to: text, inputChanged: bool]], inputsChanged: list[record[name: text, hidden: bool, from: text, to: text]], moved: list[record[name: text, from: text, to: text]], otherChanged: list[record[name: text, keys: list[text]]], fileChanged: list[text], reordered: bool]
ENVELOPE before and after are any well-formed text; a text that is not JSON is in scope (R1). Inside a parsed file every JSON
  value is in scope, with these exceptions, which are OUT: a record or map entry (TABLE DISPLAY) that is text; a list or a
  map, or a number that is not finite, in a position read by TEXT-OF (TABLE DISPLAY rows bool and bigint, a record field name,
  a RAW result composed inside a list, map or record); a RAW display result (TABLE DISPLAY rows f64, i64, u64, bytes, and
  TEXT-OF of a text) or a text case name or file or case key that holds a lone surrogate; and a JSON text nested deeper than 2100
  levels (the too-deep boundary of R2 at 2000 is IN). Nothing in scope throws.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Tell a reviewer what a change did to an oracle (test-vector) file: given the file's text before and after, report
  each file's shape and list the cases added, removed, whose expected answer was rewritten, whose inputs changed, and that
  moved between the visible (frozen) and hidden (heldout) sets; the cases whose other keys changed; the file's own keys that
  changed; and whether the common cases were reordered. Each reported case carries its displayed name and, in the first four
  lists, whether it is hidden and its input and expected values rendered for a human, every control character named. Field
  order: the result {before, after, added, removed, rewritten, inputsChanged, moved, otherChanged, fileChanged, reordered};
  added and removed {name, hidden, input, to}; rewritten {name, hidden, input, from, to, inputChanged}; inputsChanged {name,
  hidden, from, to}; moved {name, from, to}; otherChanged {name, keys}.
  A rewritten answer is the finding that stops a review.
BEHAVIOR
R1 PARSE. Each argument is read as one JSON text by the PINNED grammar JSON-GRAMMAR. A text that does not match it is the
  shape "unparseable" and contributes no cases; it is not an error. The shapes, in the order they are decided:
  "unparseable", "unknown-shape", "too-deep", "malformed-cases", "ok". In a parsed map a repeated key keeps its LAST value at
  the position of its FIRST occurrence (positions matter only to KEY-ORDER). A number is the double nearest its decimal
  value; one beyond the double range is an infinity of its sign; a value that rounds to zero keeps its sign ("-0" is
  negative zero). ABSENT below means a map has no such key; it is distinct from none.
R2 SHAPE. A parsed value is a map whose own keys "frozen" and "heldout" both hold lists ("__proto__" is an ordinary key), or
  it is the shape "unknown-shape" (none, a bool, a number, a text, a list, a map missing either list or holding something
  else there) and contributes no cases and no FILE keys. A conforming map whose DEPTH exceeds 2000 is the shape "too-deep"
  and likewise contributes no cases and no FILE keys; DEPTH counts the map itself as level 0 and every value under an own
  key of a map, or an element of a list, as one level below its container, over the whole file (any value at level 2001 or
  deeper, a scalar or an empty list included, makes it too-deep; "too-deep" wins over "malformed-cases") CITED FIDELITY package — so no compared value reaches R14's marker
  Otherwise a conforming map is the shape "ok" when every element of both lists
  is a map, and "malformed-cases" when any element is not (none, a bool, a number, a text, a list); such an element is
  skipped and the other cases are still read. FILE = the map's own keys other than "frozen", "heldout" and "canonicalSha256";
  an "unparseable" or "unknown-shape" file has no FILE keys.
R3 CASES: the map elements of "frozen" in order, then those of "heldout". A case has KEY and NAME (R4); hidden, false from
  "frozen" and true from "heldout"; ARGS, its key "args" as written (ABSENT when missing); EXPECTED, its key "expected", or
  ABSENT; and OTHER, its own keys other than "name", "args" and "expected".
R4 KEY and NAME. ID = (whether the case has the key "name", and when it has, KEY-TEXT of its value, R14). The case is the
  k-th in case order (frozen then heldout) with its ID, and KEY = (ID, k): so 1 and "1" differ, a missing name differs from
  none, 0 from -0, an infinity from none, and no literal name can equal a numbered one. Two names are the same ID exactly
  when their KEY-TEXTs are the same text. NAME, the displayed name: the name itself when it is text, its JSON-TEXT
  when it is another value, "(no name)" when the key is missing; then, for k >= 2, " (#" k ")". Displayed names may repeat
  (a literal "(no name)" or "x (#2)"); keys never do CITED FIDELITY package — keyed by (name as JSON, presence, occurrence)
R5 RESULT. LOOKUP(K) = BEFORE's case with KEY K, or nothing. For each case c of AFTER in case order:
  (a) LOOKUP(c.KEY) is nothing -> added gets {c.NAME, c.hidden, INPUT(c), SHOWN(c.EXPECTED)}.
  (b) otherwise, with o = LOOKUP(c.KEY), each of the following that holds appends, in this order: EQUAL(o.ARGS, c.ARGS)
  fails -> inputsChanged gets {c.NAME, c.hidden, INPUT(o), INPUT(c)}; EQUAL(o.EXPECTED, c.EXPECTED) fails -> rewritten gets
  {c.NAME, c.hidden, INPUT(c), SHOWN(o.EXPECTED), SHOWN(c.EXPECTED), inputChanged = whether the args comparison failed};
  o.hidden differs from c.hidden -> moved gets {c.NAME, "hidden" or "visible" for o, the same for c}; CHANGED-KEYS(o.OTHER,
  c.OTHER) is not empty -> otherChanged gets {c.NAME, those keys}.
  Then for each case of BEFORE in case order whose KEY is the KEY of no AFTER case: removed gets {its NAME, its hidden, its
  INPUT, SHOWN of its EXPECTED}. fileChanged = CHANGED-KEYS(BEFORE's FILE, AFTER's FILE). reordered is true when the KEYs
  common to both files, in BEFORE's case order, are not the same sequence as in AFTER's case order. before and after are the
  two shapes (R1, R2).
  INPUT(case): ARGS a list -> SHOWN of each element, in order, joined by ", " (a comma and a space); otherwise "(args: "
  then SHOWN(ARGS) then ")" — an ABSENT ARGS shows "(args: undefined)".
R6 EQUAL(X, Y), each a value or ABSENT: when both X and Y are CANONICAL (R7) -> CANON-EQUAL (R8); otherwise -> KEY-TEXT(X)
  is the same text as KEY-TEXT(Y) (R14) CITED FIDELITY package — canonicalString's throw falls back to keyOf; kept by ruling
R7 CANONICAL. Visit X and, recursively, every element of a visited list and every value of a visited map, except: of an
  F64-NODE (a map whose key t is the text "f64") only t and bits are visited; of a THROW-NODE (a map whose t is "throw")
  only t. X is CANONICAL when no visited position is ABSENT, none or a number; every F64-NODE's bits is a text of exactly 16
  characters from 0-9 and a-f; and no visited position lies more than 416 levels below X (X is level 0; an element or a map
  value is one level below its container) CITED FIDELITY package — the 4 x 100 + 16 raw-depth cap of canonicalString; kept by ruling
R8 CANON-EQUAL: texts are equal when they are the same code units; bools by value; lists of the same length elementwise; an
  F64-NODE equals exactly the F64-NODEs with the same bits; a THROW-NODE equals exactly the THROW-NODEs; any other two maps
  are equal when they have the same key set and CANON-EQUAL values under every key; values of different kinds are never
  equal. So map key order is immaterial, while an F64-NODE's v, a THROW-NODE's msg and any further key of either node are
  ignored CITED FIDELITY package — a throw message and an f64's v are display hints, by ruling
R9 JSON-TEXT(X): none -> "null"; a bool -> "true" or "false"; a finite number -> NUMBER-TEXT; an infinite number -> "null";
  a text -> QUOTE; a list -> "[" then the elements' JSON-TEXT joined by "," then "]"; a map -> "{" then, in KEY-ORDER,
  QUOTE(key) ":" JSON-TEXT(value), joined by ",", then "}". No whitespace is ever inserted.
R10 SHOWN(X), X a value or ABSENT: D = DISP(X) by TABLE DISPLAY, or the text "?" when DISP FAILS anywhere inside it, at any
  depth. When D is not text, or is exactly the text "?", it is replaced by JSON-TEXT(X), or by the text "undefined" when X is
  ABSENT. The result is D after CONTROL-NAMING (R11); SHOWN never throws. A "?" nested inside a larger display stays.
  DISP yields text or a RAW value (a value or ABSENT taken verbatim from the file). Composing a part
  into a list uses JOIN: text itself, ABSENT and none -> the empty text, a bool or number -> TEXT-OF. Composing a part into
  a map or a record uses TEXT-OF: text itself, ABSENT -> "undefined", none -> "null", a bool -> "true" or "false", a finite
  number -> NUMBER-TEXT CITED FIDELITY package — host renderings, kept by ruling
TABLE DISPLAY — DISP(X), first matching row; v, msg and bits name X's keys; e[0], e[1] are an entry's first two elements, ABSENT when it is shorter
  X is ABSENT or none                                    | FAILS
  X is a bool, a number, a text or a list                | "?"
  X is a map whose t is none of the texts below          | "?" (t ABSENT, not text, or another text)
  t "none"                                               | "null"
  t "undefined"                                          | "undefined"
  t "bool"                                               | TEXT-OF(v)
  t "bigint"                                             | TEXT-OF(v) then "n"
  t "text"                                               | JSON-TEXT(v); RAW ABSENT when v is ABSENT
  t "f64", "i64", "u64" or "bytes"                       | v itself, RAW (ABSENT when missing); at the top, a v that is not text, or is "?", falls back by R10
  t "throw"                                              | "throw(" then JSON-TEXT(msg) then ")"; msg ABSENT or none counts as the empty text
  t "list" or "tuple"                                    | v not a list -> FAILS; else "[" then each element's DISP by JOIN, joined by ",", then "]"
  t "map"                                                | v not a list, or an entry that is none, a bool, a number or a map -> FAILS; else "{" then per entry TEXT-OF(DISP(e[0])) ":" TEXT-OF(DISP(e[1])), joined by ",", then "}"
  t "record"                                             | v not a list, or an entry that is none, a bool, a number or a map -> FAILS; else "{" then per entry FIELD(e[0]) ":" TEXT-OF(DISP(e[1])), joined by ", ", then "}"
R11 CONTROL-NAMING, two passes over D. A CONTROL is a code unit U+0000 to U+001F or U+007F to U+009F; LABEL(c) is U+2039,
  then the TABLE NAMES label of c or else "U+" and c's four hex digits in uppercase, then U+203A. Pass 1: every occurrence
  of a backslash, the letter u and four characters from 0-9 and a-f (lowercase only) whose value is a CONTROL is replaced by
  the LABEL of that value; other occurrences stay. Pass 2: every CONTROL code unit left in the text is replaced by its
  LABEL. QUOTE's two-character escapes for backspace, tab, line feed, form feed and carriage return hold no CONTROL and stay
  as written. A text holding a backslash then "u001b", or the label text itself, displays like a real ESC; R6 still tells
  them apart CITED FIDELITY package — literal spoofing of the display, kept by ruling
TABLE NAMES — the CONTROL and its label; every other CONTROL is labelled by its code
  U+0000 | NUL
  U+0007 | BEL
  U+0009 | a backslash then t
  U+000A | a backslash then n
  U+000D | a backslash then r
  U+001B | ESC
  U+009B | CSI
R12 FIELD(n), a record field name: a text matching IDENT -> itself; any other text -> QUOTE(n); ABSENT -> "undefined"; none
  -> "null"; a bool -> "true" or "false"; a finite number -> NUMBER-TEXT; a map -> JSON-TEXT(n) CITED FIDELITY package
R13 CHANGED-KEYS(X, Y), two maps: the keys of either, de-duplicated and sorted by UTF-16 code units, whose OWN values differ by
  KEY-TEXT, a key missing from a map reading as ABSENT-MARK, a mark equal only to itself ("__proto__" and "constructor" are
  ordinary keys, never read through any inheritance).
R14 KEY-TEXT(X), a value's identity as text, a TYPED encoding: none -> "z"; ABSENT -> "u"; a number -> "n" then "-0" for
  negative zero, else NUMBER-TEXT or "Infinity" / "-Infinity"; a text -> "s" then QUOTE; a bool -> "b" then "true" or
  "false"; a list -> "[" then the elements' KEY-TEXTs joined by "," then "]"; a map -> "{" then, in KEY-ORDER, QUOTE(key)
  ":" KEY-TEXT(value), joined by ",", then "}". Every value carries its kind, so no value spells another; map key order
  counts except for index-like keys. A position nested more than 2000 levels below X (X at level 0, an element or map value
  one level below its container) is written as the marker "!deep" whatever it holds, so two values that differ only there
  have the same KEY-TEXT; R2's too-deep shape keeps every compared value of an "ok" or "malformed-cases" file above it, so the
  marker is never reached CITED FIDELITY package — keyOf's depth marker, made unreachable by the depth walk
PINNED grammar JSON-GRAMMAR = RFC 8259 JSON-text, exactly: optional whitespace (space, tab, LF, CR only), one value, optional whitespace, end. A value is "null", "true", "false", a number, a string, an array or an object; a number is an optional "-", then "0" or a digit 1-9 followed by digits, then optionally "." and one or more digits, then optionally "e" or "E", an optional sign and one or more digits; a string is a double quote, then characters at or above U+0020 other than double quote and backslash, or an escape: a backslash then one of double quote, backslash, slash, b, f, n, r, t, or a backslash, u and four hex digits of either case (a lone surrogate escape included), then a double quote. Nothing else parses: no byte-order mark, no trailing comma, no comment, no single quote, no NaN or Infinity, no leading "+" or zero, no empty text. CITED R1
PINNED grammar QUOTE = a double quote; then each UTF-16 code unit of the text in order: double quote -> backslash then double quote; backslash -> two backslashes; U+0008, U+0009, U+000A, U+000C, U+000D -> a backslash then b, t, n, f, r respectively; any other code unit below U+0020, and a lone surrogate code unit, -> backslash, u, and its four hex digits in lowercase; every other code unit -> itself (U+007F to U+009F, U+2028, U+2029 and all non-ASCII included); then a double quote. CITED R9 R11 R12
PINNED order KEY-ORDER = first every key that is an array index — the decimal spelling, with no leading zero unless it is "0", of an integer from 0 to 4294967294 — in ascending numeric order; then every other key in the order of its first occurrence in the parsed text. CITED R9
PINNED grammar NUMBER-TEXT = the shortest round-trip decimal of a finite double, as ECMAScript Number::toString with radix 10: zero of either sign -> "0"; a negative value -> "-" then the text of its magnitude; otherwise with s the shortest digit string (the one nearest the value on a tie) and n its decimal exponent so the value is 0.s times 10^n: when the digit count k <= n <= 21 the digits then n - k zeros; when 0 < n <= 21 the digits with "." after the first n; when -6 < n <= 0 "0." then -n zeros then the digits; otherwise the first digit, "." and the rest when k > 1, then "e", "+" or "-", and the decimal of n - 1 without sign (1e21 -> "1e+21", 1e-7 -> "1e-7"). CITED R4 R9 R10 R12 R14
PINNED charset IDENT = an ASCII letter or underscore, then zero or more ASCII letters, digits or underscores, over the whole name. CITED R12
INVARIANT the KEYs of one file are distinct; every case in rewritten, inputsChanged, moved or otherChanged has its KEY in
  both files, every case in added only in AFTER, every case in removed only in BEFORE; inputChanged is true exactly when the
  same AFTER case also produced an inputsChanged entry.
INVARIANT the result depends on before and after only through their parsed values: whitespace and escape spelling in the
  JSON texts never change it, and map key order changes it only through KEY-TEXT (R6's fallback, a non-text name, CHANGED-KEYS)
  and JSON-TEXT (a displayed value, R10).
NOTE rust is omitted. Cause: domain — the review system ships as JavaScript (rdv review and the rederive/sir-review GitHub Action); there is no Rust consumer.
ORACLE value -> oracles/oracle_diff.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 8e5957121e5ead1befa9b7902bd4a7bee1e74701441b2a50cb54f45999e1cace
SCOPE IN: R1-R14 over ENVELOPE. Declared by ruling: canonicalSha256 is not compared (graders check it); junk elements make
  the shape malformed-cases, a Stop, and are not itemised; a change can be reported in several lists; ABSENT shows
  "undefined"; names apart by KEY-TEXT may display alike (0 and -0 both "0"; none and an infinity both "null"), as a
  removed plus an added of one displayed name; a value shown through its JSON-TEXT (R10) writes -0 as "0" and an infinity
  as "null", so a reported rewrite of [-0, an infinity] may read "[0,null]". OUT: the other review functions of the module (explain,
  explainDiff, wordDiff) and the ENVELOPE exclusions.
PROVENANCE rederive@4c134cacd842 (MIT), cli/lsp/explain.mjs: oracleDiff, shown, NAMES, nameOf, keyOf, same, ownVal; cli/sir/canon.mjs:
  display, canonicalString (unchanged since c509956a8f8a). Driven through selfhost/seams/oracle_diff.mjs as
  oracleDiff(beforeJson, afterJson). Re-cut 2026-11-02 on the architect's round-5 rulings.
